WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.201
<i>- Previously on "The Ark"...</i>

2
00:00:02.336 --> 00:00:03.705
- Their message sounded
like they're in trouble.

3
00:00:03.771 --> 00:00:05.239
<i>- You're heading down there.</i>
<i>Is it safe?</i>

4
00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:07.509
- Of course it's safe.
<i>- They're all dead.</i>

5
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:09.577
<i>- Admiral Ben Hammond</i>
<i>of "Ark 4."</i>

6
00:00:09.644 --> 00:00:12.479
- Meet Milan Petrovic.
head of Homebase 1 security.

7
00:00:12.547 --> 00:00:14.181
<i>- I believe Kelly Fowler</i>
<i>should be freed.</i>

8
00:00:14.248 --> 00:00:15.917
<i>- I have to go out</i>
<i>in the exploration zone.</i>

9
00:00:15.984 --> 00:00:17.952
<i>You want to come?</i>

10
00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:19.587
- We're pinned down
and won't be able to make it

11
00:00:19.654 --> 00:00:21.321
to the shuttle.
- "Pinned down"? By what?

12
00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:22.790
- Alien bloody creatures. mate.

13
00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:24.424
[both scream]

14
00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:26.059
I'm gonna pull 'em off
this way.

15
00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:28.596
- No!

16
00:00:28.663 --> 00:00:31.633
- Brice!
- Run!

17
00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:35.603
- I'm so sorry. Eva.
but he's dead.

18
00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:43.277
[indistinct chatter]

19
00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:46.480
<i>[somber music]</i>

20
00:00:46.548 --> 00:00:53.487
<i>♪ ♪</i>

21
00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:58.325
- I wish we had a funeral
for Lieutenant Brice

22
00:00:58.392 --> 00:01:01.596
like we did for Doc Morozov.

23
00:01:01.663 --> 00:01:03.430
- Brice always thought
he was gonna die young

24
00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:06.066
from Klampkins and made it
very clear he didn't want us

25
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.269
wallowing in grief for him.

26
00:01:09.336 --> 00:01:12.406
- And yet here we are.

27
00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.976
- Well. I mean. I didn't know
him as well as you lot did.

28
00:01:15.043 --> 00:01:17.612
but Brice always seemed like
a "raise a glass of scotch

29
00:01:17.679 --> 00:01:21.381
and celebrate me" kind of guy.
- No. no.

30
00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:24.686
A whiskey from the homeland
tasted like piss.

31
00:01:24.752 --> 00:01:25.753
[chuckles]

32
00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.889
- Which do you think
he hated more.

33
00:01:27.956 --> 00:01:30.058
Scotch or William Trust?

34
00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.459
[laughter]

35
00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:39.601
<i>- Hammond to senior staff.</i>
<i>Report to the bridge.</i>

36
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.537
- Can he do that.
just call a meeting?

37
00:01:42.604 --> 00:01:45.039
- Apparently. he thinks he can.

38
00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:48.475
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

39
00:01:53.715 --> 00:01:57.018
- [sighs] So I've been
reviewing your logs

40
00:01:57.085 --> 00:01:58.886
and I--

41
00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:04.491
What are you looking at?

42
00:02:04.559 --> 00:02:05.893
- Uh. sir.

43
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.130
<i>♪ ♪</i>

44
00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:12.232
- Sorry. it was
the only free cabin.

45
00:02:12.299 --> 00:02:14.267
Your lieutenant
was the same size.

46
00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.672
As I said. I read your logs.

47
00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:19.607
and I want to set course
for the Tyson system.

48
00:02:19.674 --> 00:02:22.275
- Wait. hold on.
Who's in charge?

49
00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:24.545
- [chuckles]
Sharon. obviously.

50
00:02:24.612 --> 00:02:28.248
- I respect your
field promotion. Captain.

51
00:02:28.315 --> 00:02:30.018
but I still outrank you.

52
00:02:30.084 --> 00:02:32.920
- In what?
The GSA is gone.

53
00:02:32.987 --> 00:02:34.555
- Excuse me?

54
00:02:34.622 --> 00:02:36.691
- You know
the GSA charter. Admiral.

55
00:02:36.758 --> 00:02:39.661
It clearly states we were to
get humanity to new homeworlds

56
00:02:39.727 --> 00:02:42.797
and relinquish control
to civilian governance.

57
00:02:42.864 --> 00:02:45.633
<i>♪ ♪</i>

58
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.635
- Then how does this all work?

59
00:02:47.702 --> 00:02:52.239
- "Ark 1" is crewed by a mix
of former GSA and EF volunteers

60
00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:54.742
who've chosen to follow
Captain Garnet.

61
00:02:54.809 --> 00:02:56.878
- "Ark 1" is now
a Homebase 1 ship.

62
00:02:56.944 --> 00:02:58.813
not a GSA ship.

63
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.250
<i>♪ ♪</i>

64
00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.086
- I'm sorry I overstepped.

65
00:03:05.153 --> 00:03:07.220
It won't happen again.
- Good.

66
00:03:09.157 --> 00:03:10.658
- But if I may?

67
00:03:12.994 --> 00:03:15.429
Did you know that
when Evelyn Maddox fled

68
00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.066
Homebase 1. she set a course
in the shuttle nav system?

69
00:03:18.132 --> 00:03:19.399
- Yes.

70
00:03:19.466 --> 00:03:20.968
- So why haven't you
investigated?

71
00:03:21.035 --> 00:03:23.171
- I thought it was
more important to make contact

72
00:03:23.236 --> 00:03:24.337
with the Ross colony.

73
00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:26.406
- Even though
I warned you not to come?

74
00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:28.408
- Well. that's a weird way
to say thanks.

75
00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:30.444
- "Ark 1's" mission
is to save people at risk

76
00:03:30.511 --> 00:03:32.046
and bring them to Homebase.

77
00:03:32.113 --> 00:03:33.413
- Okay. and don't you think

78
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.348
there might be people
where Maddox was going?

79
00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:37.151
- Yeah. people
that want to kill us.

80
00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.353
- Or people who are suffering.
just as the people of Homebase

81
00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:43.558
were suffering
before you intervened.

82
00:03:43.624 --> 00:03:46.226
- So you want
to investigate now?

83
00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:52.399
- Tyson 5E.
It can't support life.

84
00:03:52.466 --> 00:03:54.836
- So it's likely
a rendezvous point.

85
00:03:54.902 --> 00:03:56.070
- Exactly.

86
00:03:56.137 --> 00:03:58.305
Whoever Maddox was meeting

87
00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:00.808
might still be there
waiting for her.

88
00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:04.344
We can drop out of FTL here.
right behind Tyson's moon.

89
00:04:04.411 --> 00:04:06.446
- To hide us
from any ships in orbit.

90
00:04:06.514 --> 00:04:08.015
- Solid plan.

91
00:04:11.485 --> 00:04:14.021
Okay. let's set a course.

92
00:04:14.088 --> 00:04:19.160
<i>♪ ♪</i>

93
00:04:19.227 --> 00:04:20.628
Admiral. a moment?

94
00:04:22.864 --> 00:04:25.533
You read my logs.
I read your files.

95
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.535
I appreciate your experience.

96
00:04:27.602 --> 00:04:31.038
- But not enough to follow me?
- No.

97
00:04:31.105 --> 00:04:34.542
But you'd clearly be an asset.

98
00:04:34.609 --> 00:04:36.343
I could use a number two.

99
00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:41.849
<i>♪ ♪</i>

100
00:04:41.916 --> 00:04:44.852
[thudding]

101
00:04:48.122 --> 00:04:50.892
[person grunting]

102
00:04:50.958 --> 00:04:52.292
- Kelly?

103
00:04:52.359 --> 00:04:56.097
Where are--
[gasps]

104
00:04:56.164 --> 00:04:58.199
What am I wearing?

105
00:04:58.266 --> 00:05:00.168
- I don't know.

106
00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:02.503
- You changed my clothes?
- No.

107
00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.437
I just woke up a minute ago.

108
00:05:04.505 --> 00:05:05.907
[thudding]

109
00:05:05.973 --> 00:05:08.242
- Why aren't you wearing
these weird clothes?

110
00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:10.044
- Again. I don't know.

111
00:05:10.111 --> 00:05:11.512
We were in the woods.

112
00:05:11.579 --> 00:05:15.683
There was a flash of light.
and here we are.

113
00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.185
[grunts]

114
00:05:17.251 --> 00:05:19.253
[thudding]

115
00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:21.823
[watch beeps]

116
00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:24.225
- No signal.

117
00:05:24.292 --> 00:05:25.425
Okay.

118
00:05:25.492 --> 00:05:29.230
<i>[tense music]</i>

119
00:05:29.297 --> 00:05:32.499
- My implants are on. and
I'm not even making a scratch.

120
00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:35.468
- There's no doors.
How did we get in here?

121
00:05:35.536 --> 00:05:37.238
- What I want to know is...

122
00:05:37.305 --> 00:05:40.107
<i>♪ ♪</i>

123
00:05:40.174 --> 00:05:42.977
How do we get out?

124
00:05:43.044 --> 00:05:45.847
<i>[uplifting music]</i>

125
00:05:45.913 --> 00:05:52.854
<i>♪ ♪</i>

126
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.074
Watch Online Movies and Series for FREE
www.osdb.link/lm

127
00:06:02.997 --> 00:06:05.633
- Captain Garnet.
Ian and I have a report on--

128
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.035
- Crewman. brief me first.

129
00:06:08.102 --> 00:06:09.570
- Oh.
- Seriously?

130
00:06:09.637 --> 00:06:11.739
- Chain of command.

131
00:06:11.806 --> 00:06:14.909
As her second. it's my job to
make sure her life is easier.

132
00:06:14.976 --> 00:06:17.111
- "Her second"?

133
00:06:17.178 --> 00:06:19.647
- Thank you. but we do things
a little differently here.

134
00:06:19.714 --> 00:06:21.649
They can report to me directly.

135
00:06:21.716 --> 00:06:25.152
- Captain. I gave up sunlight
and fresh air at Homebase

136
00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:27.420
to take orders from you.
- And I'm still in command.

137
00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:31.158
- But you gave Brice's job
to some guy who hitched a ride?

138
00:06:34.161 --> 00:06:37.464
- Let's all remember.
the admiral earned his rank.

139
00:06:37.531 --> 00:06:41.035
We can benefit
from his experience.

140
00:06:41.102 --> 00:06:43.470
- My training is
in peaceful missions.

141
00:06:43.537 --> 00:06:45.673
same as Captain Garnet.

142
00:06:47.742 --> 00:06:50.311
Is that good enough
for you. crewman?

143
00:06:50.378 --> 00:06:53.480
<i>[tense music]</i>

144
00:06:56.717 --> 00:06:58.586
You have a report
for the captain?

145
00:06:58.653 --> 00:07:02.056
- Oh. uh. yeah.
um. we've been analyzing

146
00:07:02.123 --> 00:07:04.058
the data on the solar shields
we created at Ross.

147
00:07:04.125 --> 00:07:05.693
I think we can make them
sustainable.

148
00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.094
- Yeah. with a bit of work.

149
00:07:07.161 --> 00:07:08.863
we can calibrate them
to protect us

150
00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.564
from different wavelengths
of radiation

151
00:07:10.631 --> 00:07:12.133
and electromagnetic pulses.

152
00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:15.002
- And maybe even small impacts
from solid matter

153
00:07:15.069 --> 00:07:16.504
like asteroid fragments.

154
00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.339
- How about impact
from artillery?

155
00:07:18.406 --> 00:07:20.508
- Well. what ever happened
to peaceful missions?

156
00:07:20.574 --> 00:07:21.943
- I want peace.

157
00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:24.211
Doesn't mean
everyone else does.

158
00:07:28.082 --> 00:07:30.985
[indistinct chatter]

159
00:07:37.525 --> 00:07:38.960
- Uh-oh.

160
00:07:39.026 --> 00:07:40.261
I know that look.

161
00:07:40.328 --> 00:07:41.896
- This?

162
00:07:41.963 --> 00:07:44.497
This is my normal.
handsome. everyday look.

163
00:07:44.565 --> 00:07:46.767
- It's your
"dying to say something

164
00:07:46.834 --> 00:07:49.737
but biting your tongue" look.

165
00:07:49.804 --> 00:07:51.372
- No. it's not my place.

166
00:07:51.439 --> 00:07:53.174
I want to respect
your boundaries.

167
00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:57.044
- Ha. says the man trying
to nickname me Curly

168
00:07:57.111 --> 00:07:58.746
because he dreamed it.

169
00:07:58.813 --> 00:08:02.416
- Yeah. about that...

170
00:08:02.482 --> 00:08:04.185
I think I understand now

171
00:08:04.251 --> 00:08:07.922
why you think our time
in the coma wasn't real.

172
00:08:07.989 --> 00:08:11.058
I spent decades
learning astrophysics. right?

173
00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:13.427
Now it turns out
it was just my brain

174
00:08:13.493 --> 00:08:16.163
creating fake science.

175
00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.933
So what else
did my brain make up?

176
00:08:21.502 --> 00:08:23.704
- I appreciate that.

177
00:08:23.771 --> 00:08:26.173
But I still want to know
what's on your mind.

178
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.442
- Isn't it obvious?

179
00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:29.910
I don't trust Hammond.

180
00:08:29.977 --> 00:08:31.779
- Because he tried
to take command?

181
00:08:31.846 --> 00:08:34.915
- Because he backed down
too easily.

182
00:08:34.982 --> 00:08:38.219
I mean. does that sound
like any admiral you ever met?

183
00:08:38.285 --> 00:08:44.025
<i>♪ ♪</i>

184
00:08:47.561 --> 00:08:52.133
- Leaving FTL in three. two...

185
00:08:52.199 --> 00:08:54.468
now.

186
00:08:54.535 --> 00:08:57.938
- [scoffs] Well. you cut that
a little close. Kimi.

187
00:08:58.005 --> 00:09:00.608
- Plan called for us to get as
near to the moon as possible.

188
00:09:00.674 --> 00:09:02.143
- And we got here in one piece.

189
00:09:02.209 --> 00:09:05.012
Call that good work.
Lieutenant Joma.

190
00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:07.081
- Passive scans only. Alicia.

191
00:09:07.148 --> 00:09:10.351
Let's try not to alert anyone
to our presence.

192
00:09:10.418 --> 00:09:13.721
- It might be
a little bit late for that.

193
00:09:13.788 --> 00:09:16.590
- It's "Ark 22."
They have energy weapons.

194
00:09:16.657 --> 00:09:18.392
- You remember what I said
about people

195
00:09:18.459 --> 00:09:20.561
wanting to kill us?
- Can your shield stop them?

196
00:09:20.628 --> 00:09:22.596
- No. we're still
upgrading them.

197
00:09:22.663 --> 00:09:24.331
- We're being hailed.

198
00:09:24.398 --> 00:09:25.633
- Charge FTL fast.

199
00:09:25.699 --> 00:09:27.101
Send them to my screen.

200
00:09:27.168 --> 00:09:32.440
<i>♪ ♪</i>

201
00:09:32.507 --> 00:09:34.642
<i>- Oh.</i>

202
00:09:34.708 --> 00:09:36.444
<i>Smart hiding place.</i>

203
00:09:36.511 --> 00:09:38.446
<i>Didn't work but smart.</i>

204
00:09:38.513 --> 00:09:41.015
<i>Let me guess--you're looking</i>
<i>for Evelyn Maddox?</i>

205
00:09:41.082 --> 00:09:41.949
- Not exactly.

206
00:09:42.016 --> 00:09:44.051
I'm Captain Sharon Garnet
of "Ark 1."

207
00:09:44.118 --> 00:09:46.454
<i>- Kurtis Drake.</i>
<i>leader of "Ark 22."</i>

208
00:09:46.521 --> 00:09:48.889
- We were investigating
why Evelyn Maddox may--

209
00:09:48.956 --> 00:09:51.125
<i>- You're a clone.</i>

210
00:09:51.192 --> 00:09:54.728
<i>you and the gentleman</i>
<i>at your side.</i>

211
00:09:54.795 --> 00:09:57.231
- Uh. what makes you think
I'm a clone?

212
00:09:57.298 --> 00:10:00.768
<i>- [chuckles] Well. because</i>
<i>I'm married to you.</i>

213
00:10:00.835 --> 00:10:05.206
<i>I'm married to one of you.</i>
<i>- [giggles]</i>

214
00:10:05.272 --> 00:10:09.210
<i>- This is. um. Jill.</i>
<i>- Hi. there.</i>

215
00:10:09.276 --> 00:10:11.412
<i>Is it conceited to say</i>
<i>you're very beautiful?</i>

216
00:10:11.479 --> 00:10:15.983
<i>Even in those drab colors.</i>
<i>[laughter]</i>

217
00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.587
<i>- Oh. and we do have J6-82s</i>
<i>on board as well.</i>

218
00:10:18.686 --> 00:10:21.889
<i>Richard. Clark.</i>
<i>come and say hi.</i>

219
00:10:21.956 --> 00:10:24.559
<i>- Oh.</i>
<i>Hello. brother.</i>

220
00:10:24.625 --> 00:10:25.826
<i>Hey.</i>
<i>- Yeah.</i>

221
00:10:25.893 --> 00:10:27.661
- Did you know there were
other clones of you?

222
00:10:27.728 --> 00:10:29.263
- N--

223
00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:31.632
<i>- Come on over.</i>
<i>It'd be lovely to have you.</i>

224
00:10:31.699 --> 00:10:34.503
- No. we don't give up
our people.

225
00:10:34.569 --> 00:10:38.272
<i>- You misunderstand.</i>
<i>This is not a request.</i>

226
00:10:38.339 --> 00:10:41.208
- He's charging weapons.

227
00:10:41.275 --> 00:10:42.843
<i>- Fire on my mark.</i>

228
00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:46.280
<i>Three. two. one.</i>

229
00:10:46.347 --> 00:10:48.482
- All right. we'll go.
- We'll come. We'll come over.

230
00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:49.984
<i>[Jill sighs]</i>
<i>- Good.</i>

231
00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.719
<i>I look forward to it.</i>

232
00:10:51.785 --> 00:10:54.355
<i>[Jill giggles]</i>
<i>Don't take too long.</i>

233
00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.594
- No. you can't go over there.

234
00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.428
- We don't have a choice.

235
00:11:03.497 --> 00:11:06.568
Admiral Hammond has command
while I'm gone.

236
00:11:06.635 --> 00:11:07.835
- Really?

237
00:11:07.902 --> 00:11:10.605
- Those are my orders.

238
00:11:10.671 --> 00:11:12.173
Keep this ship safe.

239
00:11:12.239 --> 00:11:15.176
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

240
00:11:15.242 --> 00:11:17.144
<i>♪ ♪</i>

241
00:11:17.211 --> 00:11:19.246
- This could be
a good thing. right?

242
00:11:19.313 --> 00:11:21.482
I mean. means there's
more of us in the universe.

243
00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:23.951
- Who have a whole lot
of weapons pointed at us.

244
00:11:24.018 --> 00:11:26.120
- Yeah. there is that.

245
00:11:27.855 --> 00:11:29.123
- I don't like this.

246
00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:32.259
We should have hidden weapons
in the shuttle.

247
00:11:32.326 --> 00:11:34.762
- Weapons or not.
they're outnumbered.

248
00:11:34.828 --> 00:11:36.864
<i>- Clear of "Ark 1."</i>

249
00:11:36.931 --> 00:11:39.200
- Godspeed. Captain.

250
00:11:39.266 --> 00:11:41.402
"Ark 1." out.

251
00:11:41.468 --> 00:11:45.574
- Okay. so what's your plan
to get them home?

252
00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.609
- Is the FTL charged?

253
00:11:47.676 --> 00:11:49.877
- Yeah. but--

254
00:11:49.944 --> 00:11:51.378
[ship whirs. pulses]

255
00:11:51.445 --> 00:11:55.550
- Ugh.
I really don't like that guy.

256
00:11:55.617 --> 00:11:58.687
<i>[tense music]</i>

257
00:12:03.123 --> 00:12:05.859
<i>[somber music]</i>

258
00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.662
<i>♪ ♪</i>

259
00:12:07.728 --> 00:12:10.798
[knock on door]

260
00:12:10.864 --> 00:12:13.535
<i>♪ ♪</i>

261
00:12:13.602 --> 00:12:16.504
[knocking continues]

262
00:12:16.571 --> 00:12:23.578
<i>♪ ♪</i>

263
00:12:23.645 --> 00:12:25.746
- It's a little early. Felix.

264
00:12:25.813 --> 00:12:29.651
- Yes. well. I've been trying
to reach you since last night.

265
00:12:29.718 --> 00:12:32.253
- My WD is off.
What do you need?

266
00:12:32.319 --> 00:12:33.821
- Um...

267
00:12:33.887 --> 00:12:41.028
<i>♪ ♪</i>

268
00:12:44.131 --> 00:12:46.467
- You're watching video
of Brice's death?

269
00:12:46.534 --> 00:12:48.402
- Garnet sent me the footage

270
00:12:48.469 --> 00:12:50.838
from the shuttle's
hull cameras.

271
00:12:50.904 --> 00:12:52.206
- Have you slept?

272
00:12:54.408 --> 00:12:56.745
Don't torture yourself
like this. Eva.

273
00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:59.213
- I have to see it.

274
00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.882
- May I ask you
to take a break?

275
00:13:01.949 --> 00:13:04.451
I have something to show you.

276
00:13:04.519 --> 00:13:05.754
Please.

277
00:13:05.853 --> 00:13:09.089
<i>♪ ♪</i>

278
00:13:09.156 --> 00:13:11.959
It's a new concept.
- Okay. what it is?

279
00:13:12.026 --> 00:13:13.327
- There they are.

280
00:13:15.396 --> 00:13:16.631
- Come here.

281
00:13:16.698 --> 00:13:23.638
<i>♪ ♪</i>

282
00:13:23.705 --> 00:13:25.372
- I'm sorry.

283
00:13:25.439 --> 00:13:29.143
<i>♪ ♪</i>

284
00:13:29.209 --> 00:13:32.246
- I don't normally like people
invading my personal space.

285
00:13:32.313 --> 00:13:34.582
However.
given the circumstances...

286
00:13:34.649 --> 00:13:37.951
<i>♪ ♪</i>

287
00:13:38.018 --> 00:13:40.555
He was a good man. I guess.

288
00:13:40.622 --> 00:13:43.525
- So what did you have
to show me. Felix?

289
00:13:43.591 --> 00:13:45.627
- That you are not alone.

290
00:13:45.694 --> 00:13:48.295
<i>[tender music]</i>

291
00:13:48.362 --> 00:13:49.564
- Thank you.

292
00:13:49.631 --> 00:13:50.898
- Sit down.

293
00:13:50.964 --> 00:13:54.502
<i>♪ ♪</i>

294
00:13:54.569 --> 00:13:55.969
- You eaten?

295
00:13:56.036 --> 00:13:58.640
You should eat.
- Stop mothering her.

296
00:13:58.707 --> 00:14:00.441
- I'm doctoring her.

297
00:14:00.508 --> 00:14:03.143
- I could definitely eat.
Who are we waiting for?

298
00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.647
- [sighs] Angus.
I couldn't get a hold of him.

299
00:14:05.714 --> 00:14:06.914
- Neither could I.

300
00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:08.516
- Well. I'm not gonna
wait for him.

301
00:14:08.583 --> 00:14:10.484
I'm gonna go
and get some breakfast.

302
00:14:10.552 --> 00:14:12.520
- You do that.

303
00:14:12.587 --> 00:14:15.222
- Hey. you want some coffee?

304
00:14:18.626 --> 00:14:22.129
- Who do you think put us here?
- [grunts]

305
00:14:22.196 --> 00:14:24.498
I'm more worried
about what put us here.

306
00:14:24.566 --> 00:14:25.533
- Yeah. you have a point.

307
00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.334
These walls are too smooth
to be natural.

308
00:14:27.401 --> 00:14:29.236
They had to have been
machine-honed.

309
00:14:29.303 --> 00:14:32.239
which means someone
pretty advanced built these.

310
00:14:32.306 --> 00:14:36.343
God. I just--I wonder about
the light from these crystals.

311
00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.912
Is it electric.
naturally occurring.

312
00:14:38.979 --> 00:14:40.582
filtered through
from some different--

313
00:14:40.648 --> 00:14:42.049
Can you stop that?

314
00:14:42.116 --> 00:14:43.818
You said
it's not making a dent.

315
00:14:43.884 --> 00:14:45.486
Kelly. I said stop--

316
00:14:47.287 --> 00:14:50.958
Man. Kelly.
you're trashing your hand.

317
00:14:53.628 --> 00:14:56.196
- It doesn't hurt that much.

318
00:14:56.263 --> 00:14:59.333
- You're obviously in pain.
Take a break.

319
00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.570
- It's not my hand that hurts.
It's my ears.

320
00:15:02.637 --> 00:15:05.540
With my implants on.
I hear this awful noise.

321
00:15:05.607 --> 00:15:06.741
- Turn the implants off.

322
00:15:06.808 --> 00:15:08.777
Besides. you don't want to go
into organ failure.

323
00:15:08.843 --> 00:15:12.379
- Whoever took us will be back.
I need to be ready to fight.

324
00:15:12.446 --> 00:15:15.015
- I'll fight them off till
you get your implants back on.

325
00:15:15.082 --> 00:15:18.553
- [chuckles]
That's funny.

326
00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.688
Oh. you're serious.

327
00:15:20.755 --> 00:15:22.557
- Turn them off.

328
00:15:22.624 --> 00:15:23.658
[watch beeps]

329
00:15:26.059 --> 00:15:27.695
Better?

330
00:15:27.762 --> 00:15:29.496
- I guess.

331
00:15:29.564 --> 00:15:32.132
At least I'm not hearing
that screeching sound anymore.

332
00:15:32.199 --> 00:15:35.035
[piercing tone]
[gasps]

333
00:15:36.937 --> 00:15:39.841
Something's wrong.

334
00:15:39.908 --> 00:15:41.676
I'm still hearing those sounds.

335
00:15:41.743 --> 00:15:44.579
- Wait. I hear them. too. now.

336
00:15:44.646 --> 00:15:48.182
Wait. That's what they sounded
like with your implants on?

337
00:15:48.248 --> 00:15:50.885
It's not that bad.

338
00:15:50.951 --> 00:15:53.555
[piercing tone intensifies]
Aah!

339
00:15:53.621 --> 00:15:55.422
Ah. strike that.

340
00:15:59.861 --> 00:16:02.530
- And so I think we should take
a moment of silence

341
00:16:02.597 --> 00:16:05.733
for James Brice
and Dr. Morozov.

342
00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:09.002
<i>[gentle music]</i>

343
00:16:09.069 --> 00:16:11.906
<i>♪ ♪</i>

344
00:16:11.972 --> 00:16:13.974
- Now we must move forward.

345
00:16:14.041 --> 00:16:17.512
I'm afraid we have
some more troubling news.

346
00:16:17.579 --> 00:16:21.215
Two of Homebase 1's citizens
are missing.

347
00:16:21.281 --> 00:16:25.219
Angus Medford and Kelly Fowler
went to the expanded zone

348
00:16:25.285 --> 00:16:26.588
and never returned.

349
00:16:26.654 --> 00:16:28.088
We lost contact.

350
00:16:28.155 --> 00:16:30.224
- You know what
probably happened.

351
00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:32.527
Kelly went psycho
and killed her ex-boyfriend.

352
00:16:32.594 --> 00:16:34.394
- Let's not jump
to conclusions.

353
00:16:34.461 --> 00:16:37.231
- I spent 3 years
on "Ark 15" with her.

354
00:16:37.297 --> 00:16:39.968
We can't trust her.
- It's true!

355
00:16:40.033 --> 00:16:41.669
[all murmuring]

356
00:16:41.736 --> 00:16:43.036
- Stop.

357
00:16:43.103 --> 00:16:46.641
These speculations
about Ms. Fowler end now.

358
00:16:46.708 --> 00:16:48.910
She's proven
herself repeatedly.

359
00:16:48.977 --> 00:16:51.813
- What's important
is to find them.

360
00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.749
- I'm taking volunteers
for a search party.

361
00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:58.520
Thank you.

362
00:16:58.586 --> 00:17:01.088
We'll leave after
our last order of business.

363
00:17:01.154 --> 00:17:03.791
- It's no secret that.
with the arrival

364
00:17:03.858 --> 00:17:06.761
of "Ark 1" and "EF2."

365
00:17:06.828 --> 00:17:11.331
has led to some growing
problems in our colony.

366
00:17:11.398 --> 00:17:16.103
Our council has appointed
a new sheriff. Milan Petrovic.

367
00:17:16.169 --> 00:17:20.240
[cheers and applause]

368
00:17:20.307 --> 00:17:24.979
Some of you worked
with Milan on "Ark 11"

369
00:17:25.045 --> 00:17:26.681
when he was head of security.

370
00:17:26.748 --> 00:17:30.250
We're sure that he will do
as well at this position.

371
00:17:30.317 --> 00:17:33.253
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

372
00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:40.260
<i>♪ ♪</i>

373
00:17:40.327 --> 00:17:43.430
- [breathing rapidly]

374
00:17:45.967 --> 00:17:47.869
- Sanji?
Sanji. what's wrong?

375
00:17:47.936 --> 00:17:50.304
- It's him.
- Him--who?

376
00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:52.072
- The sheriff.
- What?

377
00:17:52.139 --> 00:17:54.374
- He's my ex-husband.

378
00:17:59.079 --> 00:18:00.915
Hey. hey. are you crazy?

379
00:18:00.982 --> 00:18:02.316
- Sanji. I'm gonna talk to him.

380
00:18:02.382 --> 00:18:03.551
- No.
- Sanji!

381
00:18:03.618 --> 00:18:05.987
- No. I need to handle
this myself.

382
00:18:06.054 --> 00:18:07.589
- No. I'll go.
I'll talk to him.

383
00:18:07.655 --> 00:18:10.290
- Just give me
a moment to think.

384
00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:15.395
- Hello. Sanji.
- Stay away from her.

385
00:18:15.462 --> 00:18:18.231
- Damir.
- Let me talk to him.

386
00:18:18.298 --> 00:18:19.968
- I'll handle this.
- Sanji.

387
00:18:20.034 --> 00:18:21.368
- Alone!

388
00:18:23.236 --> 00:18:24.639
- Okay.

389
00:18:24.706 --> 00:18:26.874
I'll be in the dorm
if you need me.

390
00:18:26.941 --> 00:18:33.982
<i>♪ ♪</i>

391
00:18:34.048 --> 00:18:36.450
- [sniffs]
It's good to see you.

392
00:18:38.886 --> 00:18:42.890
Um. can we talk somewhere
more private?

393
00:18:42.957 --> 00:18:44.926
- Here's fine.

394
00:18:44.993 --> 00:18:47.795
- Fair enough.
Um...

395
00:18:47.862 --> 00:18:49.764
I owe you an apology.

396
00:18:49.831 --> 00:18:53.101
<i>♪ ♪</i>

397
00:18:53.166 --> 00:18:55.703
I'm sober now.

398
00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:59.841
I know that doesn't excuse
anything. but...

399
00:18:59.907 --> 00:19:03.745
I want you to know
I'm not the man I was.

400
00:19:03.811 --> 00:19:06.881
- Good.

401
00:19:06.948 --> 00:19:10.985
I'm not the woman I was either.

402
00:19:11.052 --> 00:19:13.554
So leave me the hell alone.

403
00:19:33.541 --> 00:19:38.146
- If you go near Sanji
ever again. I will end you.

404
00:19:38.211 --> 00:19:41.115
You hear me. you piece of shit?

405
00:19:41.181 --> 00:19:44.052
- Loud and clear.

406
00:19:44.118 --> 00:19:46.821
What. you gonna hit me.
or you gonna let me go?

407
00:19:46.888 --> 00:19:48.990
What's it gonna be. hmm?

408
00:19:49.057 --> 00:19:52.126
<i>[tense music]</i>

409
00:19:52.192 --> 00:19:54.128
[chuckles]

410
00:19:54.194 --> 00:19:56.097
Good to meet you. Damir.

411
00:19:56.164 --> 00:20:03.303
<i>♪ ♪</i>

412
00:20:04.872 --> 00:20:06.808
- [sighs]

413
00:20:09.911 --> 00:20:10.978
- For someone so concerned
with GSA protocols...

414
00:20:12.245 --> 00:20:14.015
- I can't believe you just
deserted Captain Garnet...

415
00:20:14.082 --> 00:20:15.583
[overlapping speech]

416
00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:17.484
- That's a direct violation
of GSA codes.

417
00:20:17.552 --> 00:20:20.220
I guarantee you
no one will stand for it.

418
00:20:20.287 --> 00:20:22.924
- Are you finished. or are you
just taking a breath?

419
00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:24.859
- [scoffs]
- How can you joke right now?

420
00:20:24.926 --> 00:20:27.195
- I'm actually trying
to keep my patience

421
00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:29.429
as you voice your concerns.

422
00:20:29.496 --> 00:20:31.766
- You abandoned
Captain Garnet and Ian.

423
00:20:31.833 --> 00:20:33.935
- You heard
the Captain's orders.

424
00:20:34.001 --> 00:20:37.939
She said keep the ship safe.
- Yeah. not run away.

425
00:20:38.005 --> 00:20:40.474
- Ms. Nevins. your shields
still aren't repaired.

426
00:20:40.541 --> 00:20:42.375
- What does that
have to do with--

427
00:20:42.442 --> 00:20:44.078
- And we don't
have any weapons.

428
00:20:44.145 --> 00:20:46.246
- We weren't under attack.
- Drake had what he wanted.

429
00:20:46.313 --> 00:20:48.482
There was no reason to assume
that he wouldn't open up fire.

430
00:20:48.549 --> 00:20:50.317
- And there's no reason
to assume that he would.

431
00:20:50.383 --> 00:20:52.887
- I won't risk the lives of
the entire ship for two people!

432
00:20:52.954 --> 00:20:55.123
- We just risked this entire
ship to save you on Ross.

433
00:20:55.189 --> 00:20:56.724
- And that was a bad move!

434
00:20:56.791 --> 00:20:59.560
You lost your doctor
and your number two.

435
00:20:59.627 --> 00:21:01.896
The math doesn't work.

436
00:21:01.963 --> 00:21:05.398
- People aren't math.

437
00:21:05.465 --> 00:21:07.568
- Sometimes they are.

438
00:21:11.404 --> 00:21:15.042
On Ross. Garnet traded
two lives to save one.

439
00:21:15.109 --> 00:21:17.612
Math.

440
00:21:17.678 --> 00:21:19.446
She put me in charge
because she knew

441
00:21:19.514 --> 00:21:21.249
I wouldn't make
the same mistake.

442
00:21:21.314 --> 00:21:24.552
- Or you're happy she's gone...

443
00:21:24.619 --> 00:21:27.054
so you can have this ship.

444
00:21:27.121 --> 00:21:28.890
- [scoffs]

445
00:21:28.956 --> 00:21:31.959
- Given the stress
of what's happened.

446
00:21:32.026 --> 00:21:34.729
I'm gonna pretend
I didn't hear that...

447
00:21:34.796 --> 00:21:36.363
Lieutenant Joma.

448
00:21:36.429 --> 00:21:39.366
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

449
00:21:39.432 --> 00:21:45.405
<i>♪ ♪</i>

450
00:21:47.975 --> 00:21:49.811
- Thank you so much
for coming over.

451
00:21:49.877 --> 00:21:51.979
- Yeah. well.
we didn't have a choice.

452
00:21:52.046 --> 00:21:53.514
- I never would have fired.

453
00:21:53.581 --> 00:21:56.050
Yeah. but sometimes
a show of force keeps people

454
00:21:56.117 --> 00:21:57.985
from getting the wrong idea.

455
00:21:58.052 --> 00:22:00.453
- Oh. I see.
and the armed guards--

456
00:22:00.521 --> 00:22:02.957
what kind of idea is
that supposed to give us?

457
00:22:03.024 --> 00:22:04.926
- Oh. just a precaution.

458
00:22:04.992 --> 00:22:07.494
I-I wanted to greet you
privately.

459
00:22:07.562 --> 00:22:10.998
but my wife--
yeah. she worries.

460
00:22:11.065 --> 00:22:13.333
- WDs. please.

461
00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:14.936
- Is that necessary?

462
00:22:15.002 --> 00:22:17.872
- We'll keep them safe
in our security lockup.

463
00:22:20.842 --> 00:22:26.047
<i>♪ ♪</i>

464
00:22:26.113 --> 00:22:28.716
- Can you roll up your sleeves?
- Back off.

465
00:22:28.783 --> 00:22:32.086
- It's okay.
We don't use J6-82s for organs.

466
00:22:32.153 --> 00:22:35.422
- I have a name. and it's Ian.
- Excellent.

467
00:22:35.488 --> 00:22:37.124
I encourage you even
to take a last name.

468
00:22:37.191 --> 00:22:38.391
- What's that for?

469
00:22:38.458 --> 00:22:41.128
- We need blood samples
to make sure you're not Gen-3s.

470
00:22:41.195 --> 00:22:42.530
- Well. we're not.

471
00:22:42.597 --> 00:22:44.866
Otherwise. we would have
already developed psychosis.

472
00:22:44.932 --> 00:22:46.033
- That's not true.

473
00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:48.636
There are the occasional
late bloomers.

474
00:22:48.703 --> 00:22:50.938
Easy way or hard way--
up to you.

475
00:22:51.005 --> 00:22:55.576
<i>♪ ♪</i>

476
00:22:55.643 --> 00:22:58.079
You two don't seem to know
much about clone science.

477
00:22:58.145 --> 00:22:59.379
- We know enough.

478
00:22:59.446 --> 00:23:01.115
Original humans
are Generation 1.

479
00:23:01.182 --> 00:23:02.550
and us clones are Gen-2s.

480
00:23:02.617 --> 00:23:04.952
- And clones made
from Gen-2 DNA are Gen-3.

481
00:23:05.019 --> 00:23:06.087
- Very good.

482
00:23:06.153 --> 00:23:09.489
And Gen-3 brain cells
are unstable.

483
00:23:09.557 --> 00:23:12.093
and they do develop psychosis.

484
00:23:12.159 --> 00:23:13.261
They're dangerous.

485
00:23:13.327 --> 00:23:15.529
which is why we have
to make sure you aren't one.

486
00:23:15.596 --> 00:23:17.464
- How do you know
Evelyn Maddox?

487
00:23:17.531 --> 00:23:19.432
- Evelyn was
a business acquaintance.

488
00:23:19.499 --> 00:23:22.603
She sold me this Ark
during Earth's last days.

489
00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:25.806
Joke's on her.
What does money matter now?

490
00:23:25.873 --> 00:23:28.276
- Why were you meeting her?
- I have no idea.

491
00:23:28.342 --> 00:23:32.246
She called me. but something
tells me she. um...

492
00:23:32.313 --> 00:23:34.649
she won't be showing up
just now. will she?

493
00:23:34.715 --> 00:23:36.617
- [scoffs] You could have told
us that over comms.

494
00:23:36.684 --> 00:23:38.386
- Well. then I wouldn't have
the pleasure

495
00:23:38.451 --> 00:23:40.154
to meet you lovelies in person.

496
00:23:40.221 --> 00:23:41.454
- What do you want from us?

497
00:23:41.522 --> 00:23:43.557
- It's not what
I want from you.

498
00:23:43.624 --> 00:23:45.192
It's what you want from me.

499
00:23:45.259 --> 00:23:48.195
<i>[eerie music]</i>

500
00:23:48.262 --> 00:23:51.565
<i>♪ ♪</i>

501
00:23:51.632 --> 00:23:54.702
- Ian?
- Oh. I see it.

502
00:23:54.769 --> 00:23:56.570
I don't quite believe it.

503
00:23:56.637 --> 00:23:58.773
- This ship is a safe haven
for clones.

504
00:23:58.839 --> 00:24:01.676
- Wait. are you saying everyone
on this ship is a clone?

505
00:24:03.878 --> 00:24:05.813
- It's great. isn't it?

506
00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:09.050
Guys. how are you?
[both laugh]

507
00:24:09.116 --> 00:24:10.918
Thank you so much for dinner.
- Ah! [scoffs]

508
00:24:10.985 --> 00:24:13.788
- No. it was delicious.
It was perfect.

509
00:24:13.854 --> 00:24:16.390
- I never imagined
so many clones survived Earth.

510
00:24:16.456 --> 00:24:18.225
It's amazing.

511
00:24:18.292 --> 00:24:20.761
- Yeah. I mean. besides
my brothers in the lab.

512
00:24:20.828 --> 00:24:22.930
I've never even met
another clone like me.

513
00:24:22.997 --> 00:24:25.900
- Same. I always thought
it was just me and Denise.

514
00:24:25.967 --> 00:24:28.836
I wonder how many
of each Gen-2 models were made.

515
00:24:28.903 --> 00:24:30.071
- A lot.

516
00:24:30.137 --> 00:24:32.640
The original humans designed
clones to be exploited--

517
00:24:32.707 --> 00:24:34.942
you know. labor. parts.
experimentation.

518
00:24:35.009 --> 00:24:36.844
- So they wouldn't go
to all that expense

519
00:24:36.911 --> 00:24:38.879
only to create a few of us.
- That's right.

520
00:24:38.946 --> 00:24:41.349
Cloning was a business.
and if they hadn't cut corners

521
00:24:41.415 --> 00:24:42.950
by using Gen-2s
to create Gen-3s.

522
00:24:43.017 --> 00:24:44.819
the business
would have never been outlawed.

523
00:24:44.885 --> 00:24:46.554
- I do have to admit.
it does make a nice change

524
00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.622
to see clones walking around
with smiles on their faces.

525
00:24:48.689 --> 00:24:50.691
- So this is the part
where you thank me

526
00:24:50.758 --> 00:24:53.327
for saving you from that ship.
- Saving us?

527
00:24:53.394 --> 00:24:55.029
She was the freaking captain.

528
00:24:55.096 --> 00:24:56.697
- Of a GSA Ark?

529
00:24:56.764 --> 00:24:58.666
They haven't exactly
been allies

530
00:24:58.733 --> 00:25:00.034
to the clone cause. have they?

531
00:25:00.101 --> 00:25:01.969
- The GSA doesn't exist
anymore. mate.

532
00:25:02.036 --> 00:25:04.305
We've got a new colony now.
Homebase 1.

533
00:25:04.372 --> 00:25:06.907
It's pretty nice. too.
and clones are welcome there.

534
00:25:06.974 --> 00:25:09.043
- And valued.
just like anyone else.

535
00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:11.579
just like we are welcome
on "Ark 1."

536
00:25:11.645 --> 00:25:14.048
- Oh. okay.

537
00:25:14.115 --> 00:25:16.283
Then why did they
leave you behind?

538
00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:23.290
<i>♪ ♪</i>

539
00:25:26.327 --> 00:25:27.995
- Working on a rescue plan?

540
00:25:28.062 --> 00:25:30.164
- If the admiral wants math.
I'll give him math.

541
00:25:30.231 --> 00:25:33.200
- I've served under men
like Hammond before.

542
00:25:33.267 --> 00:25:35.236
He's not changing his mind.

543
00:25:35.302 --> 00:25:37.938
- I wish Lieutenant Brice
was still here.

544
00:25:38.005 --> 00:25:40.274
What gives him the right
to decide how we proceed?

545
00:25:40.341 --> 00:25:43.044
- Garnet did
when she left him in charge.

546
00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:46.814
- [scoffs] Well. he'll change
his tune when I show him this.

547
00:25:46.881 --> 00:25:50.317
- I hope so.
but. um. just in case...

548
00:25:50.384 --> 00:25:51.986
here's a plan B.

549
00:25:53.988 --> 00:25:56.323
It's always good to have
a backup plan.

550
00:25:58.759 --> 00:26:00.628
- Are you searching
for inhabitable worlds

551
00:26:00.694 --> 00:26:02.029
to settle on?

552
00:26:02.096 --> 00:26:04.098
- Who's to say
we haven't found one?

553
00:26:04.165 --> 00:26:07.034
- Is that your old man?
- Oh. no. no.

554
00:26:07.101 --> 00:26:09.504
That's. um. Kurtis Drake prime.

555
00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:12.139
the inventor
of cloning science.

556
00:26:12.206 --> 00:26:15.109
But in a way. he's the--
he's the father of us all.

557
00:26:15.176 --> 00:26:17.044
- You're his clone.

558
00:26:17.111 --> 00:26:21.248
- Yes. uh. but do not mistake
my work for the creator's.

559
00:26:21.315 --> 00:26:24.652
He sought wealth.
- And what do you seek?

560
00:26:24.718 --> 00:26:27.354
- To build a place
for us to live in harmony

561
00:26:27.421 --> 00:26:30.491
without oppression
from the original humans.

562
00:26:30.559 --> 00:26:32.226
- Clone society.

563
00:26:32.293 --> 00:26:35.530
- Well. forget the fact
that your model was designed

564
00:26:35.596 --> 00:26:37.231
for the rigors of space

565
00:26:37.298 --> 00:26:39.467
or that you were made
to be an organ donor.

566
00:26:39.534 --> 00:26:41.602
Here we can be
whatever we want--

567
00:26:41.669 --> 00:26:44.506
doctors. scientists. poets.

568
00:26:44.573 --> 00:26:48.242
Here we're free.

569
00:26:48.309 --> 00:26:50.344
- We have a colony
you could join.

570
00:26:50.411 --> 00:26:53.714
- I mean. the two of you might
be accepted. but all of us?

571
00:26:53.781 --> 00:26:56.484
Old prejudices die hard.
Captain.

572
00:26:56.551 --> 00:26:59.554
<i>- Morgan to Drake.</i>
<i>Can you come to medbay?</i>

573
00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:01.755
- On my way. Doctor.

574
00:27:01.822 --> 00:27:04.458
Oh. here's the mess hall.

575
00:27:04.526 --> 00:27:06.060
The food is delicious.

576
00:27:06.127 --> 00:27:08.262
Go on in. Relax.
Have a meal.

577
00:27:08.329 --> 00:27:10.164
I'm sure you'll have
more questions later.

578
00:27:10.231 --> 00:27:12.933
- No guards?
- We have nothing to hide.

579
00:27:20.374 --> 00:27:23.310
[indistinct chatter]

580
00:27:23.377 --> 00:27:26.280
<i>[mysterious music]</i>

581
00:27:26.347 --> 00:27:28.249
<i>♪ ♪</i>

582
00:27:28.315 --> 00:27:30.417
- Uh...

583
00:27:30.484 --> 00:27:32.786
<i>♪ ♪</i>

584
00:27:36.257 --> 00:27:38.159
[knock on door]

585
00:27:45.232 --> 00:27:48.435
- I figured it out.
- You figured out what?

586
00:27:50.404 --> 00:27:52.173
There are a lot
of calculations on here

587
00:27:52.239 --> 00:27:54.074
that fall outside
of my expertise.

588
00:27:54.141 --> 00:27:57.411
- Alicia has a plan to save
Captain Garnet and Ian.

589
00:27:57.478 --> 00:27:59.413
- Oh.
I'm listening.

590
00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:02.349
- A while ago. William Trust
created a weaponized EM pulse.

591
00:28:02.416 --> 00:28:04.418
- According to your logs.
it nearly fried your engines.

592
00:28:04.485 --> 00:28:06.921
- Yes. but I figured out
how to re-create it

593
00:28:06.987 --> 00:28:09.490
in a way that we can fire off
three shots safely.

594
00:28:09.558 --> 00:28:12.092
- Wow.
Three shots.

595
00:28:12.159 --> 00:28:13.662
And what about your shields?

596
00:28:13.727 --> 00:28:15.062
Have you found a way

597
00:28:15.129 --> 00:28:17.198
to recalibrate them
to withstand "Ark 22" weapons?

598
00:28:17.264 --> 00:28:19.033
- No. but--
- All right. stop.

599
00:28:19.099 --> 00:28:20.801
- But if the EM
disables their weapons--

600
00:28:20.868 --> 00:28:22.303
- You're dismissed.

601
00:28:22.369 --> 00:28:25.072
- Sir. no one is happy we've
left two of our own behind.

602
00:28:25.139 --> 00:28:26.575
- We've had this conversation.

603
00:28:26.641 --> 00:28:29.109
I'm not gonna risk the lives
of the entire ship on an if.

604
00:28:29.176 --> 00:28:31.745
- Is this your final decision?
- Yes. it's my final decision.

605
00:28:31.812 --> 00:28:34.114
Now. unless you can--

606
00:28:34.181 --> 00:28:37.586
[groans]

607
00:28:37.652 --> 00:28:40.788
- I was really hoping
we wouldn't have to do plan B.

608
00:28:40.854 --> 00:28:44.593
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

609
00:28:44.659 --> 00:28:49.363
- No one in or out until
we've got our captain back.

610
00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:50.998
[keypad beeps]

611
00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:53.934
- Time is not on our side.

612
00:28:55.002 --> 00:28:56.937
If Kelly and Angus
suffered an accident.

613
00:28:57.004 --> 00:28:59.139
they'll need food. water.
and medical attention.

614
00:28:59.206 --> 00:29:01.408
We should pair up
and widen our search.

615
00:29:03.777 --> 00:29:06.180
- I'll search with you. Felix.

616
00:29:06.247 --> 00:29:08.650
- I think not.

617
00:29:08.717 --> 00:29:10.484
- [chuckles]

618
00:29:10.552 --> 00:29:13.287
Who told you. Sanji or Marsh?

619
00:29:13.354 --> 00:29:15.389
- I would have never approved
you as sheriff

620
00:29:15.456 --> 00:29:16.857
if I'd known who you are.

621
00:29:16.924 --> 00:29:18.993
- Who I am. Felix.

622
00:29:19.059 --> 00:29:23.163
is a guy who wants to help
you find two missing people.

623
00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:24.898
Hey.

624
00:29:24.965 --> 00:29:27.569
Do you want my help or not?

625
00:29:27.636 --> 00:29:30.538
<i>[eerie music]</i>

626
00:29:30.605 --> 00:29:37.545
<i>♪ ♪</i>

627
00:29:40.214 --> 00:29:43.551
[piercing tone]
- This is a torture technique.

628
00:29:45.252 --> 00:29:47.855
- My WD is picking up patterns
in the noise.

629
00:29:47.921 --> 00:29:49.957
There's harmonic variations
at frequencies

630
00:29:50.024 --> 00:29:51.726
outside the range
of human hearing.

631
00:29:51.792 --> 00:29:53.260
- So what does that mean?

632
00:29:53.327 --> 00:29:55.429
- It explains
why you could hear it

633
00:29:55.496 --> 00:29:59.233
when your implants were on.
- How is that helpful?

634
00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:02.036
- I have no idea.

635
00:30:02.102 --> 00:30:04.238
I don't know.
I don't know.

636
00:30:10.277 --> 00:30:13.280
[keypad beeping. lock clicks]

637
00:30:18.052 --> 00:30:20.354
[keys clacking]

638
00:30:20.421 --> 00:30:22.724
- Oh. Markovic.
I'm glad you're here.

639
00:30:22.791 --> 00:30:25.359
I could use your help
to recalibrate the spectrograph

640
00:30:25.426 --> 00:30:28.028
to run a chemical analysis
on the tablet.

641
00:30:28.095 --> 00:30:31.265
- Maybe later.

642
00:30:31.332 --> 00:30:32.667
- This tablet

643
00:30:32.734 --> 00:30:35.469
could be the greatest discovery
of our lifetime.

644
00:30:35.537 --> 00:30:37.137
maybe human history.

645
00:30:37.204 --> 00:30:40.174
So what could possibly be
more interesting than that?

646
00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:44.978
Well...

647
00:30:45.045 --> 00:30:46.781
maybe working on the tablet

648
00:30:46.847 --> 00:30:48.583
will help take your mind
off your grief.

649
00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:51.952
- Something happened to Brice
as the shuttle took off.

650
00:30:52.019 --> 00:30:55.189
- Eva.
- Just watch.

651
00:30:55.255 --> 00:30:58.125
First. an exhaust fume.

652
00:30:58.192 --> 00:31:00.194
and there. he's gone.

653
00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:02.196
- I don't want to be harsh.

654
00:31:02.262 --> 00:31:05.132
but. uh. something
was eating him.

655
00:31:05.199 --> 00:31:08.068
- Then where is his body?
- I don't know.

656
00:31:08.135 --> 00:31:10.904
I guess the creature
dragged it away?

657
00:31:10.971 --> 00:31:15.275
- Or he--he walked away.

658
00:31:15.342 --> 00:31:17.512
- Eva...

659
00:31:17.579 --> 00:31:19.046
I understand.

660
00:31:19.113 --> 00:31:20.515
I really do.

661
00:31:20.582 --> 00:31:23.585
I desperately miss
Helena and Cat.

662
00:31:23.651 --> 00:31:26.286
but I've had to accept
that they're gone.

663
00:31:26.353 --> 00:31:29.056
just like you have to accept
that Lieutenant Brice is gone.

664
00:31:29.123 --> 00:31:31.024
Now. come on.
[keys clacking]

665
00:31:31.091 --> 00:31:33.795
Come and help me
with the tablet. please?

666
00:31:33.862 --> 00:31:35.095
- No.

667
00:31:35.162 --> 00:31:38.465
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

668
00:31:46.206 --> 00:31:50.244
[indistinct chatter]

669
00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:52.847
- Sun's getting low.
and it's still hot as hell.

670
00:31:52.913 --> 00:31:54.181
Everyone's exhausted.

671
00:31:54.248 --> 00:31:56.551
- We can't stop
until we find them.

672
00:31:56.618 --> 00:31:57.786
- Look. I get it.

673
00:31:57.852 --> 00:31:59.687
Angus and Kelly
are your people.

674
00:31:59.754 --> 00:32:03.323
but we've been at this all day.
- They're <i>our</i> people.

675
00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:04.992
- Sure.

676
00:32:05.058 --> 00:32:06.460
But so are they.

677
00:32:06.528 --> 00:32:08.495
Look. everyone here
needs a meal

678
00:32:08.563 --> 00:32:09.597
and a good night's sleep.

679
00:32:09.664 --> 00:32:11.599
We can continue
the search tomorrow.

680
00:32:11.666 --> 00:32:14.435
Come on. man.
tell me you're not tired.

681
00:32:14.501 --> 00:32:18.205
- I'm going to keep on looking.
You can do what you want.

682
00:32:20.307 --> 00:32:23.778
- Well. you're a better man
than me. Felix.

683
00:32:23.845 --> 00:32:26.380
Okay. everyone who's ready
to call it for the night.

684
00:32:26.447 --> 00:32:27.715
follow me.

685
00:32:27.782 --> 00:32:30.117
[whistles]

686
00:32:30.184 --> 00:32:33.120
<i>[tense music]</i>

687
00:32:33.187 --> 00:32:37.291
<i>♪ ♪</i>

688
00:32:37.357 --> 00:32:39.561
- I can't take
much more of this!

689
00:32:39.627 --> 00:32:40.662
- What?

690
00:32:40.728 --> 00:32:42.897
- I said. I can't take
more of this!

691
00:32:42.963 --> 00:32:44.498
- Me either!

692
00:32:44.566 --> 00:32:47.635
[piercing tone]

693
00:32:47.702 --> 00:32:51.739
[tone stops]
[gasping]

694
00:32:53.974 --> 00:32:56.243
Maybe someone heard us?

695
00:32:58.178 --> 00:33:00.582
- Or the next torture
is about to begin.

696
00:33:00.648 --> 00:33:05.152
[rumbling]

697
00:33:05.219 --> 00:33:07.622
Get behind me.
[watch beeps]

698
00:33:07.689 --> 00:33:10.991
<i>♪ ♪</i>

699
00:33:11.058 --> 00:33:13.160
- Maybe we're
supposed to go out?

700
00:33:13.227 --> 00:33:15.429
- It could be a trap.

701
00:33:15.496 --> 00:33:20.033
<i>♪ ♪</i>

702
00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:21.903
[gas hisses]

703
00:33:21.970 --> 00:33:24.906
[both groaning]

704
00:33:24.973 --> 00:33:29.076
<i>♪ ♪</i>

705
00:33:29.142 --> 00:33:32.279
[deep rumbling]

706
00:33:35.048 --> 00:33:37.117
Oh. shit.
- Angus!

707
00:33:37.184 --> 00:33:40.287
- [grunting. shouting]

708
00:33:44.224 --> 00:33:47.160
Maybe let's stay in here.

709
00:33:49.731 --> 00:33:52.132
<i>[light music]</i>

710
00:33:52.634 --> 00:33:57.939
<i>♪ ♪</i>

711
00:33:58.006 --> 00:33:59.741
- Welcome aboard.

712
00:33:59.807 --> 00:34:01.308
- Ah.
- Thank you.

713
00:34:01.375 --> 00:34:03.176
- Happy to be here.

714
00:34:03.243 --> 00:34:05.045
Something's wrong here. right?

715
00:34:05.112 --> 00:34:07.281
- Yeah. Drake's pushing the
hard sell a little too much.

716
00:34:07.347 --> 00:34:09.216
- Hey. girl!
[giggles]

717
00:34:09.283 --> 00:34:11.586
I've totally got clothes
in your size

718
00:34:11.653 --> 00:34:14.354
if you. like.
ever want to borrow them.

719
00:34:14.421 --> 00:34:16.456
- Uh. y-you know I do.

720
00:34:16.524 --> 00:34:18.693
- [exhales sharply]
I knew it.

721
00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:20.795
<i>♪ ♪</i>

722
00:34:20.862 --> 00:34:23.031
- Have you noticed there's
no other clones of Drake here.

723
00:34:23.096 --> 00:34:24.298
but there are of everyone else?

724
00:34:24.364 --> 00:34:25.733
- Yeah. we're gonna need

725
00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:27.501
to figure out
his connection to Maddox.

726
00:34:27.569 --> 00:34:29.469
get our WDs.
and signal "Ark 1."

727
00:34:29.537 --> 00:34:30.972
- You don't really believe

728
00:34:31.039 --> 00:34:32.774
we can rely
on Hammond for help?

729
00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:34.274
- If we can make contact.

730
00:34:34.341 --> 00:34:36.076
uh. maybe we can figure out
a rescue plan

731
00:34:36.143 --> 00:34:37.745
that won't put "Ark 1"
in danger.

732
00:34:37.812 --> 00:34:39.446
- [sighs]
Fair enough.

733
00:34:39.514 --> 00:34:41.248
- [chuckles]
- Divide and conquer?

734
00:34:41.315 --> 00:34:43.250
- Yes. I'll get
into the security lockup

735
00:34:43.317 --> 00:34:44.652
and grab our WDs.

736
00:34:44.719 --> 00:34:46.219
- Right. I'll go
to the server room.

737
00:34:46.286 --> 00:34:47.956
see if there's
any communications logs

738
00:34:48.022 --> 00:34:49.456
with Maddox.
- I don't know.

739
00:34:49.524 --> 00:34:52.392
Going to the bridge
might look suspicious.

740
00:34:52.459 --> 00:34:54.596
- What about Drake's quarters?

741
00:34:54.662 --> 00:34:57.532
Maybe there's a WD there
with some information on it.

742
00:34:57.599 --> 00:34:58.967
- Good idea.

743
00:34:59.033 --> 00:35:00.868
Meet at the shuttle.
- Yeah.

744
00:35:06.007 --> 00:35:07.542
- What have you got for me?

745
00:35:07.609 --> 00:35:10.310
- I ran the blood sample
three times.

746
00:35:10.377 --> 00:35:12.680
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

747
00:35:12.747 --> 00:35:17.150
- [scoffs] We finally found
what we were looking for.

748
00:35:17.217 --> 00:35:24.358
<i>♪ ♪</i>

749
00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:30.898
[device beeps]
- [chuckles]

750
00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:40.340
- [yelps]
- [gasps]

751
00:35:40.407 --> 00:35:42.644
- You're not Clark.
- Uh. yes. I am.

752
00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:45.245
- No. you're not.

753
00:35:45.312 --> 00:35:48.482
- No. I'm not.
- [gasps]

754
00:35:48.549 --> 00:35:51.218
You're one of the clones
Kurtis freed from "Ark 1."

755
00:35:51.284 --> 00:35:53.286
- "Freed"?
[scoffs]

756
00:35:53.353 --> 00:35:55.389
Kidnapped. more like.

757
00:35:57.625 --> 00:35:59.359
Oh. no.

758
00:35:59.426 --> 00:36:01.629
No. I wouldn't do that
if I was you.

759
00:36:01.696 --> 00:36:03.831
- Kurtis is gonna throw
your ass out the airlock

760
00:36:03.898 --> 00:36:06.834
for sneaking into our quarters.
- Oh. yeah?

761
00:36:06.901 --> 00:36:09.704
And tell me. what do you think
would make Drake angrier--

762
00:36:09.771 --> 00:36:10.905
me sneaking in here

763
00:36:10.972 --> 00:36:15.677
or you dressed like that
waiting for a guy named Clark?

764
00:36:15.743 --> 00:36:19.080
- [sighs]

765
00:36:19.147 --> 00:36:20.748
What do you want?

766
00:36:20.815 --> 00:36:23.417
- What's Drake's connection
to Maddox?

767
00:36:23.483 --> 00:36:26.353
You tell me that.
and I'll leave.

768
00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:28.990
- All's I know is Maddox
was trying to help Kurtis

769
00:36:29.057 --> 00:36:30.792
find a special G-44?

770
00:36:30.858 --> 00:36:32.627
- What's a G-44?

771
00:36:32.694 --> 00:36:35.295
- [laughs]

772
00:36:35.362 --> 00:36:37.197
- You're looking at one.

773
00:36:39.433 --> 00:36:42.269
Ship full of us
and still not the one he wants.

774
00:36:44.172 --> 00:36:47.240
- Sharon.

775
00:36:47.307 --> 00:36:48.910
[door closes]

776
00:36:52.479 --> 00:36:56.918
- Oh. and you must be
the new G-44 clone.

777
00:36:56.984 --> 00:36:59.319
I'm Roger.

778
00:36:59.386 --> 00:37:01.923
- Nice to meet you.

779
00:37:04.892 --> 00:37:07.028
- Do you want to go
for a drink sometime?

780
00:37:07.095 --> 00:37:08.629
- Excuse me?

781
00:37:08.696 --> 00:37:11.199
- It's just that I find that
I'm most compatible with G-44s.

782
00:37:11.264 --> 00:37:12.767
and I don't mean to brag.

783
00:37:12.834 --> 00:37:16.537
but I usually sweep them
off their feet.

784
00:37:16.604 --> 00:37:18.072
I'll grab her legs.

785
00:37:18.139 --> 00:37:21.542
Let's get her to Dr. Morgan
before this wears off.

786
00:37:25.513 --> 00:37:28.448
- Well. I guess everyone's
done playing nice.

787
00:37:28.516 --> 00:37:30.450
<i>- Okay. listen up. team.</i>

788
00:37:30.518 --> 00:37:32.319
The moment we're out of FTL.

789
00:37:32.385 --> 00:37:34.555
the bridge will fire
an EM pulse at "Ark 22."

790
00:37:34.622 --> 00:37:36.524
Once we have confirmation
of a hit.

791
00:37:36.591 --> 00:37:38.226
we'll launch and board.

792
00:37:38.291 --> 00:37:41.562
We should expect
heavy resistance.

793
00:37:41.629 --> 00:37:43.765
<i>- Bridge to Kimi--</i>
<i>we're two minutes out.</i>

794
00:37:43.831 --> 00:37:44.699
Are you ready?

795
00:37:44.766 --> 00:37:47.835
- We're locked and loaded.
bridge.

796
00:37:47.902 --> 00:37:50.905
<i>[ominous music]</i>

797
00:37:50.972 --> 00:37:57.310
<i>♪ ♪</i>

798
00:37:57.377 --> 00:37:59.113
- Wow.

799
00:37:59.180 --> 00:38:01.348
You shouldn't be conscious.

800
00:38:01.414 --> 00:38:04.051
Our metabolism is fantastic.
isn't it?

801
00:38:04.118 --> 00:38:06.954
Triple the dosage
on the sedative.

802
00:38:07.021 --> 00:38:09.724
- [breathing heavily]

803
00:38:09.791 --> 00:38:12.560
- Don't worry.

804
00:38:12.627 --> 00:38:14.896
This won't hurt too much.

805
00:38:14.962 --> 00:38:18.566
[tool whirring]
- [grunts. breathing heavily]

806
00:38:25.673 --> 00:38:27.942
- Where's Garnet?
[breathing heavily]

807
00:38:28.643 --> 00:38:31.444
- Medbay.
- Oh. yeah?

808
00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:36.184
[breathing heavily]

809
00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:38.886
- You don't have to do this.
- Yes. I do. You're the one.

810
00:38:38.953 --> 00:38:40.254
- What does that mean?

811
00:38:40.320 --> 00:38:41.956
[tool whirring]

812
00:38:42.023 --> 00:38:43.390
- Shh.

813
00:38:43.456 --> 00:38:45.092
Go to sleep now.

814
00:38:47.161 --> 00:38:48.361
- Sharon!

815
00:39:14.121 --> 00:39:16.057
[scalpel clatters]

816
00:39:31.772 --> 00:39:32.940
- Ian!

817
00:39:37.612 --> 00:39:39.914
Let's get the hell
off this ship.

818
00:39:41.682 --> 00:39:44.018
- Sasha. you have a handle
on the EM pulse. right?

819
00:39:44.085 --> 00:39:45.620
- Yep.
- Good.

820
00:39:45.686 --> 00:39:47.454
Timing is everything on this.

821
00:39:47.521 --> 00:39:49.590
Leaving FTL

822
00:39:49.657 --> 00:39:53.160
in three. two. one...

823
00:39:53.227 --> 00:39:55.096
Fire EMP. Sasha.

824
00:39:55.162 --> 00:39:57.131
- At what?

825
00:39:57.198 --> 00:39:59.333
There's no sign of "Ark 22."

826
00:39:59.399 --> 00:40:01.002
- Oh. no.

827
00:40:03.871 --> 00:40:06.240
- We can't get a visual
on "Ark 22."

828
00:40:06.307 --> 00:40:10.111
<i>- We're too late.</i>
<i>"Ark 22" has left.</i>

829
00:40:16.317 --> 00:40:19.687
- I asked you to do one thing.

830
00:40:19.754 --> 00:40:23.291
- I know. I'm sorry.
I just saw red.

831
00:40:23.357 --> 00:40:25.393
- That--that
can't happen again.

832
00:40:25.458 --> 00:40:27.628
- It won't.
I-I promise.

833
00:40:27.695 --> 00:40:30.197
- I need to handle this my way.

834
00:40:30.264 --> 00:40:32.600
- I know.

835
00:40:32.667 --> 00:40:35.336
Last thing I would want
to do is hurt you.

836
00:40:35.403 --> 00:40:37.571
Okay?
- Okay.

837
00:40:39.273 --> 00:40:41.676
Yeah. I'm sorry.

838
00:40:41.742 --> 00:40:44.879
<i>[dramatic music]</i>

839
00:40:44.946 --> 00:40:48.316
<i>♪ ♪</i>

840
00:40:48.382 --> 00:40:52.019
Why are you looking
at Milan's file?

841
00:40:52.086 --> 00:40:54.655
- I just wanted to see
who we're dealing with.

842
00:40:54.722 --> 00:40:58.025
- <i>We</i> are not dealing with this.
<i>I</i> am.

843
00:40:58.092 --> 00:41:01.095
Why can't you understand that?
- I do.

844
00:41:01.162 --> 00:41:03.130
I do. I'm s--

845
00:41:03.197 --> 00:41:04.165
[sighs]
Sanji.

846
00:41:04.231 --> 00:41:06.667
- I need some time alone.
- Oh. come on.

847
00:41:06.734 --> 00:41:08.102
- I said I need some time.

848
00:41:08.169 --> 00:41:11.339
Or do I need to tell you
that twice. too?

849
00:41:11.405 --> 00:41:14.108
- [scoffs]

850
00:41:14.175 --> 00:41:16.944
- I tried to keep Felix
from searching too far

851
00:41:17.011 --> 00:41:21.082
from the settlement.
but he is determined

852
00:41:21.148 --> 00:41:24.018
to find Angus and Kelly.

853
00:41:24.085 --> 00:41:26.420
- You need to take this
more seriously.

854
00:41:26.486 --> 00:41:28.522
- [sighs]

855
00:41:28.589 --> 00:41:31.325
- I am taking this seriously.

856
00:41:31.392 --> 00:41:34.095
but there's nothing we can do.

857
00:41:35.963 --> 00:41:39.300
- You could have stopped Felix
from searching further.

858
00:41:39.367 --> 00:41:43.504
- Well. it's not like
he's gonna find the two kids.

859
00:41:43.571 --> 00:41:46.774
Ves. we both know
what happened to them.

860
00:41:46.841 --> 00:41:48.242
- Do we?

861
00:41:48.309 --> 00:41:50.544
Because if you're right.

862
00:41:50.611 --> 00:41:53.147
we have a much bigger problem
than we thought.

863
00:41:53.214 --> 00:41:56.150
<i>[tense music]</i>

864
00:41:56.217 --> 00:41:58.119
<i>♪ ♪</i>

864
00:41:59.305 --> 00:42:59.471
